<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  What the signed Yam.app may do under the hardened runtime (docs/project/signing.md).

  Notarization requires the hardened runtime, and the hardened runtime takes
  away, by default, everything an entitlement here gives back. So this file is
  the list of exceptions, and each one is here because something stops working
  without it. `forge.config.ts` applies it to the application and its plain
  helper; the GPU, renderer and plugin helpers keep @electron/osx-sign's own
  defaults, which are Chromium's, and narrower.

  Deliberately absent, because nothing here needs them:

  * `com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory`. Electron before
    12 needed it for V8. Electron 44 allocates JIT pages with MAP_JIT, which
    `allow-jit` covers, and the unsigned-memory exception is the broader of the
    two. If a signed build ever dies at launch with a code-signing crash in V8,
    this is the key to try first, and the crash report is the evidence.
  * `com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation`. Library validation
    means the process loads only code signed by Apple or by the same team.
    Yam.app loads its own frameworks, which the same identity signs. The bundled
    CLI in `Resources/yam` has no native Node addon (no `.node` file), and it
    never runs inside this process anyway: the app spawns it with a separate
    `node` (`nodeRuntime` in `src/main/index.ts`), whose own signature and
    entitlements govern what that process may load. Entitlements are not
    inherited by a child that is its own signed executable.
  * `com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables`. The spawned
    `node` gets its environment from the app; the app itself reads no DYLD_*.
  * Camera, microphone, location, Bluetooth, USB, printing. @electron/osx-sign
    grants all of these by default. Yam uses none, and an entitlement nobody
    uses is permission a compromised renderer could use.
  * The App Sandbox. Yam drives other applications and runs a person's project
    code; a sandboxed Yam could do neither. It is distributed with Developer ID,
    not through the Mac App Store, so the sandbox is not required.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <!--
      V8 compiles JavaScript to machine code at run time, in the main process
      (Node) and in every helper that runs JavaScript. The hardened runtime
      refuses writable-and-executable memory unless this is present, and the
      app aborts before its first window.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <!--
      The macOS accessibility adapter acts through System Events, by running
      `osascript` (packages/adapter-ax/src/bridge.ts). From the app, that
      `osascript` is a grandchild: Yam.app spawns `node`, which spawns it. TCC
      holds the *responsible* process to account, which is Yam.app, and under the
      hardened runtime an app without this entitlement is refused Apple Events
      with no prompt at all (errAEEventNotPermitted, -1743). The entitlement only
      lets macOS ask; the person still decides, in the Automation prompt.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
